Trufflow Project – Complete README
This README explains Milestone 1, Milestone 2, and Milestone 3 of the Trufflow project. The goal of Trufflow is to monitor app to app transactions and automatically detect important insights such as anomalies, spikes, and unusual behavior. This document is written in simple language for easy understanding and includes real values, figures, and tables.

Milestone 1: Problem Definition, Data Understanding, and Initial Exploration
Purpose of Milestone 1
The purpose of Milestone 1 is to clearly define the problem, understand the data, and explore basic patterns. This milestone answers the questions: What problem are we solving, what data do we have, and what does the data look like?
Problem Statement
Modern systems generate millions of transactions between applications. Manual monitoring is slow and error prone. Small issues such as rising error rates or increasing costs may go unnoticed until they become major failures. Trufflow is designed to automatically detect these issues early and present them as insights.
Project Goals
1.	Monitor app to app transactions at scale
2.	Detect abnormal behavior automatically
3.	Reduce manual investigation time
4.	Provide clear and actionable insights
Data Description
The dataset contains raw transaction logs that were aggregated into hourly windows. Each row represents one app to app interaction for one hour.
Key dataset size values:
•	Raw transactions: approximately 7.25 million
•	Aggregated hourly windows: approximately 1.54 million
•	Number of numeric features: 6
Feature Description Table
Feature Name	Description
req_count	Number of requests in the hour
error_rate	Percentage of failed requests
cost_sum	Total cost for the hour
cost_mean	Average cost per request
data_sum	Total data transferred
data_mean	Average data per request
Initial Data Exploration
In this milestone, we performed basic checks such as missing values, distributions, and simple statistics. We confirmed that:
•	No critical missing values were present
•	Some features were highly skewed, especially cost_sum and req_count
•	Different apps showed very different scales
